<article class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">FRAMEWORK REFERENCE / BETA 0.1</p><h1>What is<br/><em>EditableFrames?</em></h1><p>EditableFrames is an MIT-licensed JavaScript framework for videos created with code. A clip keeps its creative source and may expose typed controls for colors, geometry, cameras, timing or media. Stable anchors identify those details. People and tool-capable agents apply validated commands through reversible edit history. The framework does not require Remotion, a Stitchable account or a model API key.</p><p>Reference updated <time datetime="2026-10-07">7 October 2026</time>. The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/beta.md`}>beta contract in the source repository</a> defines implemented scope and remaining gaps.</p>
<h2>How do controls and edit history work?</h2><p>The scene code chooses what to draw and which details are editable. Its control schema defines valid values; stable anchor IDs identify editable nodes. Edits are validated atomically and checked against revisions. Undo and redo preserve the path through those changes. Schemas describe an editing interface, rather than a fixed vocabulary of possible visuals.</p><p>A phone scene can expose model selection, screen content and camera motion. A scientific explainer can expose labels and signal speed. New rendering libraries can connect through adapters as the code becomes more ambitious. See the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/architecture.md`}>source architecture</a> and <a href="/genres/device/">interactive 3D phone example</a>.</p>
<h2>What can you export today?</h2><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Workflow</th><th scope="col">Available output</th><th scope="col">Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Browser playground</th><td>PNG frames and JSON edit recipes</td><td>12 hosted studies; settings are specific to this playground runtime</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Local SDK</th><td>MP4 with audio; transparent ProRes 4444 MOV; PNG/WAV sequences</td><td>Supported local clip projects; hosted studies are not all CLI export templates</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An edit recipe is a settings file, not a video or a portable SDK project. Transparent exports can support overlay workflows in compatible editors. The local beta executes trusted JavaScript; it is not an untrusted-code sandbox. General HTML capture, production streaming decode and complete host-app integration remain further work. Consult the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/beta.md`}>implemented beta scope</a> before choosing a production workflow.</p>
<h2>How can Claude Code, Codex or another agent use it?</h2><p>The repository includes a model-neutral CLI and a shared SKILL.md. An agent needs a host that can read files and run local tools. A particular host integration should be tested independently; a shared skill does not establish universal plugin compatibility. Install from a source checkout because an npm package has not been published.</p><ol><li>Follow the <a href={`${repo}#try-it`}>repository setup instructions</a>.</li><li>Use the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/agents.md`}>agent setup guide</a> for the chosen host.</li><li>Create a local clip, inspect its editable anchors and preview a change.</li><li>Check history and export the supported project using the CLI.</li></ol>
<h2>How are EditableFrames and Stitchable related?</h2><p><a href="https://stitchable.ai/">Stitchable</a> is the parent company and creator of EditableFrames. <a href="https://stitchable.ai/studio/">Stitchable Pro Studio</a> is the broader desktop video-editing product. EditableFrames is a separate open-source beta with its own contract, local SDK and independent browser playground. Downloading the framework does not require a Stitchable account.</p><p><a href="/guide/">Try the browser editing guide</a> · <a href="/credits/">Review device and library licenses</a> · <a href="/#gallery">Explore the scene collection</a></p></article>
